# SQL and Migrations

## Migrations (goose, `db/migrations`)

- Append-only. Never edit a migration that has been committed; add a new one.
- Every migration has both `-- +goose Up` and `-- +goose Down`, and the Down must actually reverse the Up.
- Regenerate sqlc after any schema change (`make sqlc`, then `make sqlc-check`).

## Queries (sqlc, `db/queries`)

- Every query that filters, joins, or sorts needs an index justification: add a comment naming the index it relies on, or say why a scan is acceptable.
- Paginate lists with keyset pagination on `(published_at, id)`. Never `OFFSET`.
- Select explicit columns, not `SELECT *`.
- Use sqlc parameters (`$1`, `sqlc.arg(name)`); never build SQL by string concatenation.

```sql
-- BAD: OFFSET gets slower the deeper the page
SELECT id, slug, title FROM posts ORDER BY published_at DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000;

-- GOOD: keyset, first page when the cursor args are NULL.
-- Index: CREATE INDEX ON posts (published_at DESC, id DESC) WHERE status = 'published';
-- name: ListPosts :many
SELECT id, slug, title, published_at
FROM posts
WHERE status = 'published'
  AND (
    sqlc.narg(after_published_at)::timestamptz IS NULL
    OR (published_at, id) < (sqlc.narg(after_published_at)::timestamptz, sqlc.narg(after_id)::bigint)
  )
ORDER BY published_at DESC, id DESC
LIMIT sqlc.arg(page_size);
```

The index should be partial (`WHERE status = 'published'`) to match the query's filter. Use `sqlc.narg` for the optional cursor so page 1 needs no sentinel values.

## Search

- Use `websearch_to_tsquery`, rank with `ts_rank_cd`.
- Apply `ts_headline` only to the top N ranked rows, never the full result set (it re-parses documents).
